  <content>
  <section><title> Cool Down Activity</title>
<para>The graph shows the population of beavers in a forest for different numbers of years after 1995. The beaver population is growing exponentially.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Graph of function."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/395ccead9d495335d6acc6920d5b22ef0a27bca7"/></media></figure></para>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Explain why we can think of the beaver population as a function of time in years.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> The number of beavers depends on time in years. In any given year, there is a particular number of beavers in the forest.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> What is the meaning of the point labeled \(Q\) in this context?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> The point \(Q\) indicates that 3.5 years after 1995, the beaver population was about 550.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Write an equation using function notation to represent this situation.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> \(p(t)=50 \cdot 2^t\) where \(t\) is years after 1995 and \(p(t)\) is the beaver population at that time.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="daf41464-483b-41ba-8bb4-dbfad8888c4d" data-retry-limit="0" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <exercise data-content-id="0c20dc9a-915d-4066-ab59-64b5717b29be" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-solution="\(y=10 + 4^x\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(y=10 + (4x)^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y=10 + 4^x\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y=10 + 4x^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y=10 + 4^{\\frac{1}{x}}\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>4. Which equation is most appropriate for modeling this data?</para>
      <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
        <thead/>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">x</entry>
            <entry>1</entry>
            <entry>2</entry>
            <entry>3</entry>
            <entry>4</entry>
            <entry>5</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">y</entry>
            <entry>14</entry>
            <entry>26</entry>
            <entry>74</entry>
            <entry>266</entry>
            <entry>1034</entry>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </problem>
    </exercise>
  <solution>
    <para>Correct Answer!</para>
  </solution>
